{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01185 - MONDO_18068",
  "verbalization": "Fluoxymesterone, a synthetic steroid, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Trisomy 13 through a series of interconnected pathways involving testosterone-based therapies. The evidence suggests that Fluoxymesterone may be linked to Trisomy 13 via the treatment of Klinefelter Syndrome, a condition characterized by the presence of an extra X chromosome. Both Klinefelter Syndrome and Trisomy 13 are associated with disrupted sex hormone regulation, particularly testosterone deficiency, which may lead to developmental and physiological abnormalities. The presence of Fluoxymesterone in the treatment of Klinefelter Syndrome, and its subsequent association with Trisomy 13, indicates a potential mechanistic link where testosterone therapy could influence chromosomal stability or cellular differentiation processes.\n\nThe evidence is further supported by the role of testosterone enanthate and propionate in the pathogenesis of Trisomy 13, suggesting that the administration of testosterone-based drugs may contribute to the development of this condition. The shared indication of Trisomy 13 in multiple pathways involving testosterone therapy implies a possible biological mechanism where testosterone treatment could disrupt normal developmental processes, leading to chromosomal abnormalities. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of understanding the interplay between sex hormones and chromosomal stability, particularly in the context of developmental disorders. The evidence, while not definitive, provides a plausible framework for further investigation into the role of testosterone therapy in the etiology of Trisomy 13.",
